The InfluxDB 3 Java client (`influxdb3-java`) works the same way on Enterprise and Core. The one difference is the token: on Enterprise, give the service a token scoped to its own database instead of the admin token. Your working directory is empty, so the code is below rather than written to files. Nothing here has been compiled or run against a server.

## 1. Setup (once, server side)

```bash
# Check the server is up. A 200 with an x-influxdb-build header containing "Enterprise" is what you want.
curl -i -H "Authorization: Bearer $ADMIN_TOKEN" "$INFLUXDB_HOST/ping"

# Create the database with the admin token
influxdb3 create database sensor_data --host "$INFLUXDB_HOST" --token "$ADMIN_TOKEN"

# Create a token that can only read and write that database
influxdb3 create token --permission "db:sensor_data:read,write" \
  --name my-java-service --host "$INFLUXDB_HOST" --token "$ADMIN_TOKEN"
```

The new token is shown only once, so save it straight into your secret store. The service uses this scoped token and never sees the admin token.

