## Notes for Cloud Serverless

- **Writes:** The client writes through `/api/v2/write`, which is the only write endpoint Cloud Serverless has. One bad line rejects the whole batch. Send writes in batches of about 1,000 points, or flush every second, whichever comes first.
- **Queries:** Use SQL. Cloud Serverless doesn't run Flux. Pass any user input through `params`, never by building the query string yourself.
- **The client only needs three settings:** host, token and database (the bucket name). `INFLUXDB_ORG` only matters if you write with raw HTTP instead of the client.
- **Why there's a startup check:** Cloud Serverless has no database-management API. The code checks the bucket by running a small query with your app token, so a misspelled bucket name or a token without access stops the app with a clear error.

If you'd like, I can add write and query helpers once you tell me what your data looks like (what it measures, and which identifiers tell sources apart).

The best way to page through 10 million rows in InfluxDB 3 is by time window. Query one fixed window (for example, one hour), then move the window forward and query again. Don't use `LIMIT/OFFSET`: each page has to scan and throw away every row before it, so pages get slower as the offset grows.

Also avoid "`time > last_seen_time LIMIT n`" cursors. Many sensors can write at the same timestamp, so rows that share a timestamp at the edge of a page can be skipped. Fixed windows with `>= start AND < end` don't have that problem.

You don't need a `LIMIT` inside a window, either. The JS client streams results (over Arrow Flight), so `for await` handles one window at a time without loading all 10M rows into memory.

